          <div style="background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-xs); padding: 0.75rem;">
            <strong style="font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--text-primary); display: block;">
              Q1: Why do we use two distinct tokens (<code class="code-pill">atoken</code> vs. <code class="code-pill">btoken</code>) instead of a single JWT?
            </strong>
            <p style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; line-height: 1.45;">
              <strong>Expected Answer:</strong> <code class="code-pill">atoken</code> is an IAM identity-only token with no direct banking transaction privileges. <code class="code-pill">btoken</code> is issued strictly by Admin Console, carries core banking <code class="code-pill">apiToken</code> and session ID (<code class="code-pill">sid</code>), and is validated against an active database session and blacklist to prevent session hijacking.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div style="background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-xs); padding: 0.75rem;">
            <strong style="font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--text-primary); display: block;">
              Q2: If HTTPS already encrypts network traffic, why does the frontend fetch an RSA public key to encrypt the password before dispatch?
            </strong>
            <p style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; line-height: 1.45;">
              <strong>Expected Answer:</strong> Defense-in-depth. If TLS terminates early at a reverse proxy, load balancer, or WAF, traffic inside the internal network could expose plaintext passwords. Client RSA ensures credentials remain ciphertext end-to-end until reaching the private key in IAM.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div style="background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-xs); padding: 0.75rem;">
            <strong style="font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--text-primary); display: block;">
              Q3: Why did SDD build <code class="code-pill">sequelize-java</code> instead of using Hibernate or Spring Data JPA in Azure Functions?
            </strong>
            <p style="font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; line-height: 1.45;">
              <strong>Expected Answer:</strong> Hibernate incurs huge memory footprints (100MB+) and heavy cold-start delays. <code class="code-pill">sequelize-java</code> provides fluent SQL query generation, parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection, and starts in milliseconds with minimal serverless memory overhead.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div style="background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-xs); padding: 0.75rem;">
            <strong style="font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--text-primary); display: block;">
